---
apply_to: ["crates/**/*.rs", "src/**/*.rs"]
---

## Implementation rules

### Mock-first workflow

Design changes start in the mock workspace. Write trait signatures,
struct definitions, and module boundaries in `mock/crates/` first;
validate with `cargo check`; lock a doc changelist; then produce an
src changelist. Only after both changelists lock does code move to
real implementation paths. See `mock/WORKFLOW.md.tmpl`.

### Compiler posture — strict by default

At every crate root, deny warnings that hide real bugs:

```rust
#![deny(unused, unreachable_code, unused_must_use, unused_imports, dead_code)]
```

Strict mode is not opt-in. The assumption is that authors care about
correctness; warnings that mask mistakes stay off.

### No `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` in non-test code

- `?` propagation with tagged error enums is the default.
- Infallible paths may use `.unwrap()` only when the compiler cannot
  see the invariant (rare). In that case, attach a short `// invariant:`
  comment explaining why the unwrap is sound.
- `.expect("foo")` with a message is no better than `.unwrap()` — it
  still panics. Return a `Result` instead.

### Tagged enums + trait dispatch, not duck typing

The Python prototype leans on Protocol + `isinstance` checks. The Rust
port does not. Model variation as:

- A tagged `enum` when the set is closed (token kinds, AST nodes,
  binding forms).
- A trait with a blanket impl + newtypes when the set is open but
  known at compile time (pass registrations, validator rules).
- A trait object (`dyn Trait`) — avoid. Compile-time monomorphisation
  is the house style; `dyn` / `TypeId` / `std::any` are forbidden in
  framework code. Leaf tooling that genuinely needs heterogeneous
  storage may use `dyn`, with a design-round note explaining why.

### Descriptive errors

Every error variant carries structured context — span, phase,
expected/actual, relevant identifier — not just a message string.
Error types are tagged enums per phase (e.g. `LexError`, `ParseError`,
`ResolveError`, `TypeError`) joined by a crate-level `Diagnostic`
that carries span + severity + code.

```rust
// Bad
Error::Other("unexpected token".into())

// Good
ParseError::UnexpectedToken { span, found: Token::Arrow, expected: &[Token::Ident, Token::LParen] }
```

### No `macro_rules!` for Vehje language semantics

Vehje language macros are procedural, compile-time, and execute as a
scheduler pass using the same grammar primitives the parser uses. They
are typechecked like normal code.

`macro_rules!` inside the Rust implementation is acceptable for Rust
boilerplate (span forwarding, diagnostic code enums, test matrices).
It is never acceptable for expressing Vehje semantics — every bit of
Vehje's macro story lands in the proc-macro compiler pass.

### Reuse the substrate

Before adding a graph / DAG / bitmask / sparse / spectral / pipeline
type, check the substrate:

- DAG topology, traversal, dominators — `arvo-graph`.
- Read/write sets, artifact tracking, dense-or-sparse bitmasks —
  `arvo-bitmask`.
- Sparse containers — `arvo-sparse`.
- Pipeline morsel execution, scheduler, pools — `hilavitkutin`.

If a substrate API is close but wrong-shape, raise an issue (or a
cross-repo design round) against the substrate repo. Do not fork the
behaviour locally.

### Single-binary distribution

No dynamic runtime deps. No `dlopen` plugin loading from the compiler
layer. No reliance on an ambient Python / Deno / JVM / .NET runtime.
Extension points are added at compile time via trait impls in
extension repos (`vehje-jomini`, future), not at runtime.

### Game-agnostic core

Nothing in this repo names a specific game. If you need to reference
Clausewitz script, Witcher 3 script, Sims 4 script, or Lua — the
reference belongs in an extension repo. The base compiler is a
language front-end, not a game adapter.

### Tests and coverage

- Tests next to source in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { }` for unit
  coverage.
- Integration tests in `tests/` per crate.
- The Python prototype's test corpus at
  `~/Dev/stellar-heritage/tools/clause/` is the **parity oracle**:
  port relevant test cases to Rust, run them against the Rust
  implementation, verify semantic equivalence. Do not translate the
  Python implementation itself; translate the test intent.

### File size

Target under 300 lines per file; 400 lines is the lint cap. Split
large modules by responsibility (lexer state, token types, error
reporting as separate files rather than one `lexer.rs` behemoth).
